WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:09.183
Your tablet asks for a web page.
A computer far away answers.

2
00:00:09.403 --> 00:00:12.840
The asker is a client.
The answerer is a server.

3
00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:17.148
On a network, some devices
ask and one computer answers.

4
00:00:17.368 --> 00:00:23.173
The asker is a client: a tablet wanting
a page, a laptop wanting a file.

5
00:00:23.393 --> 00:00:28.362
The answerer is a server. It stores
things and serves them when asked.

6
00:00:28.582 --> 00:00:32.065
One server can answer hundreds
of clients, all day long.

7
00:00:32.285 --> 00:00:37.486
Is the server the biggest, or the
boss? No. It is the one that answers.

8
00:00:37.986 --> 00:00:41.980
A server is a computer whose
job is to serve other computers.

9
00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:46.240
Big networks have several
servers. Each one has its own job.

10
00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:51.708
A file server keeps everybody's files.
An application server runs programs.

11
00:00:51.928 --> 00:00:56.851
A web server holds web pages. A
print server queues the printing.

12
00:00:57.071 --> 00:01:02.133
A mail server passes messages. All
five wait to be asked, then answer.

13
00:01:02.633 --> 00:01:07.509
The internet is the network of
networks. Millions of them, joined.

14
00:01:07.729 --> 00:01:12.791
Cables under the sea, routers passing
data, servers: all joined together.

15
00:01:13.011 --> 00:01:17.469
The World Wide Web is pages, linked
to each other and read in a browser.

16
00:01:17.689 --> 00:01:22.101
The internet is the roads. The web
is one kind of traffic on them.

17
00:01:22.321 --> 00:01:27.383
Email, calls and games are not the
web. They use the internet too.

18
00:01:27.883 --> 00:01:30.855
What makes it a web, and
not just a pile of pages?

19
00:01:31.075 --> 00:01:35.301
The hyperlink. Words you
press to jump to another page.

20
00:01:35.521 --> 00:01:39.283
That page may be on another
server, in another country.

21
00:01:39.503 --> 00:01:44.332
One page points to another, until
the pages of the world are joined.

22
00:01:44.832 --> 00:01:48.780
Devices join a network in
two ways: ethernet, or wi-fi.

23
00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:52.668
Ethernet is a cable, from
your computer to the router.

24
00:01:52.888 --> 00:01:58.276
Wi-fi is wireless: no wire at all.
It carries your data on radio waves.

25
00:01:58.496 --> 00:02:01.932
Those are the same waves
that bring music to a radio.

26
00:02:02.432 --> 00:02:07.262
A wire is faster and steadier. It
carries more, with no interference.

27
00:02:07.482 --> 00:02:12.683
A wire is safer. A stranger outside
cannot join without plugging in.

28
00:02:12.903 --> 00:02:17.779
Wi-fi goes where you go: a tablet
in the garden, a phone in your hand.

29
00:02:17.999 --> 00:02:22.040
But thick walls weaken it, and
anyone in range can try to join.

30
00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:26.996
So wi-fi needs a password. That is
the trade, and a school has both.

31
00:02:27.496 --> 00:02:31.165
A client asks. A server
stores things and serves them.

32
00:02:31.385 --> 00:02:36.076
The internet is the network. The web
is the pages that travel over it.

33
00:02:36.296 --> 00:02:41.358
Ethernet is a cable: faster, safer,
and you must plug in to join.

34
00:02:41.578 --> 00:02:45.571
Wi-fi is radio: no wire,
so it goes wherever you go.
